Input: {"question": "What does the name \"Woolstone\" mean?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d00", "start": 0, "end": 240, "text": "Woolstone, Milton Keynes Great Woolstone and Little Woolstone are two historic villages in modern Milton Keynes, ceremonial Buckinghamshire now called jointly Woolstone or The Woolstones and forming the heart of a new district of that name."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d00", "start": 241, "end": 341, "text": "At the 2011 Census the population of the villages was included in the civil parish of Campbell Park."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d00", "start": 342, "end": 424, "text": "The name 'Woolstone' is an Old English language word, and means 'Wulfsige's farm'."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d00", "start": 425, "end": 492, "text": "In the Domesday Book of 1086 the area was recorded as \"Wlsiestone\"."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d00", "start": 493, "end": 628, "text": "Until shortly after the turn of the 19th century, the villages were named Woolstone Magna (Great Woolstone) and Woolstone Parva (Little"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d01", "start": 0, "end": 240, "text": "Woolstone, Milton Keynes Great Woolstone and Little Woolstone are two historic villages in modern Milton Keynes, ceremonial Buckinghamshire now called jointly Woolstone or The Woolstones and forming the heart of a new district of that name."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d01", "start": 241, "end": 341, "text": "At the 2011 Census the population of the villages was included in the civil parish of Campbell Park."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d01", "start": 342, "end": 421, "text": "The name 'Woolstone' is an Old English language word, and means 'Wolf's stone'."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d01", "start": 422, "end": 489, "text": "In the Domesday Book of 1086 the area was recorded as \"Wlsiestone\"."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d01", "start": 490, "end": 637, "text": "Until shortly after the turn of the 19th century, the villages were named Woolstone Magna (Great Woolstone) and Woolstone Parva (Little Woolstone)."}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d02", "start": 0, "end": 17, "text": "be Saxon burials."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d02", "start": 18, "end": 101, "text": "The Domesday Book of 1086 records the toponym as \"Olvricestone\" or \"Olvrichestone\"."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d02", "start": 102, "end": 188, "text": "Entries in the Book of Fees for 1242 record it as \"Wulfricheston\" and \"Wulvricheston\"."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d02", "start": 189, "end": 278, "text": "Other 13th-century spellings include \"Wulwricheston\", \"Wluricheston\" and \"Wulevycheston\"."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d02", "start": 279, "end": 434, "text": "It evolved into \"Wlfricheston\" and \"Woulricheston\" in the 14th century and \"Wolierston\" and \"Wolston\" in the 16th century before reaching its present form."}, "D3S6": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d02", "start": 435, "end": 500, "text": "The name is derived from Old English and means \"Wulfric's \"tūn\"\"."}, "D3S7": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d02", "start": 501, "end": 587, "text": "\"Tūn\" is an Old English word for \"fence\" that came to mean \"enclosure\" or \"homestead\"."}, "D3S8": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d02", "start": 588, "end": 666, "text": "The Domesday Book of 1086 records that the Bishop of Winchester held an estate"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 0, "end": 8, "text": "1994 ) ."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 9, "end": 57, "text": "Excavations on medieval sites in Milton Keynes ."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 58, "end": 129, "text": "Aylesbury : Buckinghamshire Archaeological Society , Monograph Series ."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 130, "end": 141, "text": "Mynard , D."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 142, "end": 144, "text": "C."}, "D4S6": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 145, "end": 171, "text": "; Hunt , Julian ( 1994 ) ."}, "D4S7": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 172, "end": 209, "text": "Milton Keynes , a pictorial history ."}, "D4S8": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 210, "end": 235, "text": "Chichester : Phillimore ."}, "D4S9": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 236, "end": 256, "text": "ISBN 0-85033-940-5 ."}, "D4S10": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 257, "end": 268, "text": "Mynard , D."}, "D4S11": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 269, "end": 271, "text": "C."}, "D4S12": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 272, "end": 286, "text": "; Zeepvat , R."}, "D4S13": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 287, "end": 289, "text": "J."}, "D4S14": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be69244660:d03", "start": 290, "end": 305, "text": "; Williams , R."}, "D4S15": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0291-83be6924466